================================================================================
WHAT THIS COVERS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
================================================================================

The MIT licence above covers the code in this repository and nothing else.

It does not cover the game's text or its audio. Those belong to Fantasy Flight
Games and Middle-earth Enterprises. This repository never contains them: it
publishes an extractor and a renderer, and each person runs the extraction
against their own installation for their own local, non-distributed use. The
licence notice at the top of the README is the one that matters there, and the
.gitignore is what enforces it.


THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS

None of these are included here. pip fetches each at install time, and each is
governed by its own licence rather than by this one. Read as declared by the
installed package metadata on a Mac, 2026-07-30:

  piper-tts                          GPL-3.0-or-later
  num2words                          LGPL
  UnityPy                            MIT
  rapidfuzz                          MIT
  pillow                             MIT-CMU
  ocrmac                             MIT
  pyobjc-framework-ScreenCaptureKit  MIT
  pyobjc-framework-Quartz            MIT
  onnxruntime                        MIT
  numpy                              BSD-3-Clause, and others per component

Three more install only on their own platform and so could not be read from this
machine. They are listed with what their projects declare, which is worth
confirming on a machine that has them:

  rapidocr-onnxruntime               Apache-2.0   (Windows and Linux)
  windows-capture                    MIT          (Windows)
  winrt-*                            MIT          (Windows)

**piper-tts is GPL-3.0-or-later, and this project imports it directly.** That
does not affect running it yourself, which is what this is for. It is the first
thing to read if you intend to redistribute something built on top of this,
because a combined work is not covered by the MIT above. num2words being LGPL is
the same question in a milder form.

This list is a convenience and not legal advice. Each package's own licence is
authoritative, and versions change.
